WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.666 --> 00:00:02.768
♪ (MUSIC PLAYING) ♪

00:00:03.369 --> 00:00:04.904
KATE WINSLET:<i> When I
 first read the script</i>

00:00:04.938 --> 00:00:06.606
<i> written by Brad Ingelsby,</i>

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.408
it completely
took my breath away.

00:00:08.875 --> 00:00:11.177 line:0%
Every episode ends
on a real cliffhanger.

00:00:11.577 --> 00:00:13.446
I sat up till
the early hours of the morning,

00:00:13.512 --> 00:00:14.847
reading every single episode.

00:00:15.248 --> 00:00:18.851
<i> I had never read a character
who was able to pull off so much</i>

00:00:18.884 --> 00:00:22.121
<i> in terms of how she copes,
 how she deals with people.</i>

00:00:22.521 --> 00:00:24.957
And she's extraordinary
at her job,

00:00:25.024 --> 00:00:28.861
so I felt enormous admiration
for a person like that.

00:00:29.395 --> 00:00:31.230
BRAD INGELSBY:<i> Kate wanted
 to pursue the character</i>

00:00:31.264 --> 00:00:34.633 line:0%
in a way that was lovely
as a writer to have someone

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.370 line:0%
who wants to put in the time
like that and dig deep.

00:00:37.637 --> 00:00:39.772
<i> Eventually, it became
 Kate's creation.</i>

00:00:39.805 --> 00:00:41.875
<i> I trusted her
 to make dialogue changes,</i>

00:00:41.908 --> 00:00:45.211
because I felt convinced
that she knew this character
better than I did.

00:00:45.411 --> 00:00:47.313 line:0%
Kate was constantly
adding things, I mean,

00:00:47.947 --> 00:00:50.416
<i> we would rehearse
 the scene, block the scene.</i>

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.917
<i> And then... she would say,</i>

00:00:51.951 --> 00:00:53.453
"Mare would
probably just do this."

00:00:54.387 --> 00:00:57.157
<i> Or like... "Should I
 be eating something?
 Like some junk food?"</i>

00:00:58.258 --> 00:01:01.727
<i> Like how do we make
 a new iteration of it
 that's even more Mare?</i>

00:01:02.561 --> 00:01:04.997
It's definitely been,
I'd say, one of,

00:01:05.064 --> 00:01:07.800
<i> if not the most challenging
 role I've ever played.</i>

00:01:07.833 --> 00:01:10.470
<i> Just because
 of how enormous it was,</i>

00:01:10.503 --> 00:01:13.473
<i> the sheer volume of story,
 the number of characters.</i>

00:01:13.773 --> 00:01:15.441
And then the dialect.

00:01:15.474 --> 00:01:18.310
All right. Everybody,
put your gloves on.

00:01:18.344 --> 00:01:21.914
Do a grid search. Mark anything
that seems out of place.

00:01:22.281 --> 00:01:23.616
So we can photograph it.

00:01:24.850 --> 00:01:27.419
KATE:<i> Anyone from
 Delaware County will know</i>

00:01:27.620 --> 00:01:30.956
that the dialect
is very complicated.

00:01:31.357 --> 00:01:33.793 line:0%
It's a very subtle one,
which makes it harder
to pick up.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:36.929
And it can also sound Southern
if you don't do it right.

00:01:36.963 --> 00:01:39.832 line:0%
There were certain check-in
words that I would have,
like "water."

00:01:40.099 --> 00:01:41.267
Get your water bottle ready.

00:01:41.634 --> 00:01:43.436 line:0%
The trickiest part
are the-- are the O's.

00:01:43.703 --> 00:01:46.172
(DELAWARE ACCENT)
Going home alone,
on my own.

00:01:46.539 --> 00:01:48.708
Which is very different to
"going home alone on my own."

00:01:48.975 --> 00:01:50.777
I need to walk out of here
and go home.

00:01:50.810 --> 00:01:52.145
And every once
in a while, I'd say,

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:55.214
"Brad, you've given
me seven O's in this one line!"

00:01:55.280 --> 00:01:56.850
HELEN FAHEY:<i> I don't know
 what the hell to say.</i>

00:01:57.317 --> 00:01:59.786
Oh, wait. It just came to me.
That was stupid!

00:02:01.153 --> 00:02:02.388
Goddamned idiotic!

00:02:03.655 --> 00:02:05.057
SUSAN HEGARTY:<i> If you're
 working on a dialect,</i>

00:02:05.625 --> 00:02:06.960 line:0%
you can start
with the sound changes.

00:02:07.627 --> 00:02:09.862 line:0%
You can start with
the rhythms, you can start
with certain melodies.

00:02:10.029 --> 00:02:11.196
<i> You can start anywhere.</i>

00:02:12.364 --> 00:02:15.568
You just chip away at it,
until you eventually can
pull the whole thing together.

00:02:16.001 --> 00:02:18.638
<i> Kate really does have
 this prodigious work ethic.</i>

00:02:18.671 --> 00:02:21.908
We just drilled it and worked
it, and it was constant.

00:02:22.575 --> 00:02:24.810
SUSAN:<i> She was utterly
 committed to doing this well,</i>

00:02:24.843 --> 00:02:27.880
<i> to doing it properly,
 to honoring the local culture.</i>

00:02:28.314 --> 00:02:30.349
And being able to shoot locally
like that is very helpful.

00:02:31.016 --> 00:02:34.020
CRAIG ZOBEL:<i> We were really
 trying to be as specific
 as we could to the area.</i>

00:02:34.387 --> 00:02:36.755
In addition to that, we would
like do it with costume.

00:02:37.423 --> 00:02:41.160
<i> Our costume designer
 would be texting me pictures
from the Wawa convenience store.</i>

00:02:41.193 --> 00:02:43.229
<i> Just of people in the line.</i>

00:02:43.262 --> 00:02:46.699
KATE:<i> We would spend
 so much time distressing
 every item of clothing</i>

00:02:47.099 --> 00:02:49.902
with scrubbing brushes
and putting holes in things

00:02:49.936 --> 00:02:51.938
<i> and all her clothes
 feel like clothes.</i>

00:02:52.005 --> 00:02:53.439
<i> They don't feel like costumes.</i>

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:55.775
I told the costume designer
that I wanted to wear

00:02:55.842 --> 00:02:57.610
hip pads and butt pads.

00:02:57.877 --> 00:02:59.745
And she practically cried
she was so happy.

00:02:59.778 --> 00:03:02.615
She said, "Actresses
never ask me for that!"

00:03:03.282 --> 00:03:05.084
<i> Uh, we're just asking anyone
 who knows anything</i>

00:03:05.117 --> 00:03:06.786
<i> to please come forward.</i>

00:03:08.054 --> 00:03:09.689
Maybe it's a serial killer.

00:03:10.122 --> 00:03:11.424
Christ, Mom.

00:03:11.857 --> 00:03:14.193
Well, I mean,
it is the third girl, Mare.

00:03:14.227 --> 00:03:15.528
♪ (SUSPENSEFUL MUSIC PLAYS) ♪

00:03:16.062 --> 00:03:19.198
CRAIG:
<i>A thing that became clear to me
 while making the show</i>

00:03:19.231 --> 00:03:22.802
<i>was that we were really talking
about people who'd been affected</i>

00:03:22.868 --> 00:03:26.305
by trauma, trying to learn
to move on from their trauma.

00:03:26.939 --> 00:03:29.241
ANGOURIE RICE:<i> There's a scene
 where Siobhan comes home.</i>

00:03:29.608 --> 00:03:33.246
That's when all of the things
that she hasn't been able to say

00:03:33.279 --> 00:03:35.781
or hasn't been allowed to say,
come out.

00:03:35.814 --> 00:03:38.050
-You don't even talk about him.
-MARE SHEEHAN: What are you
talking about, Siobhan?

00:03:38.083 --> 00:03:39.818
-Come on. Hey, come on--
-You don't even talk about him
anymore.

00:03:39.885 --> 00:03:41.120
And I hate you for that,
I hate you!

00:03:41.153 --> 00:03:42.722
You should have been the one
to find him.

00:03:42.755 --> 00:03:44.791
-MARE: Stop!
-Not me, not me!

00:03:45.491 --> 00:03:48.828
When I first read that scene
in the script, I just cried.

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:50.062
Sometimes as an actor,

00:03:50.096 --> 00:03:52.731
it's just horrible
shooting certain scenes.

00:03:53.065 --> 00:03:54.767
And that scene
was very upsetting

00:03:54.801 --> 00:03:56.802
and, uh, extremely painful.

00:03:57.169 --> 00:03:59.171
Kate and Angourie and I
had a conversation

00:03:59.605 --> 00:04:01.907
<i> about what is going
 to make us feel</i>

00:04:01.941 --> 00:04:03.842
<i> like we've achieved
 a real moment.</i>

00:04:03.909 --> 00:04:07.113
Maybe have less people on set
and not necessarily have

00:04:07.146 --> 00:04:08.681
the time pressure
that we would have

00:04:08.747 --> 00:04:10.683
in a different scenario.

00:04:10.749 --> 00:04:13.319
It was very, kind of,
intimate and personal.

00:04:13.686 --> 00:04:15.821
<i> It was just heartbreaking
 to film.</i>

00:04:15.988 --> 00:04:18.691
It was very hard
to differentiate

00:04:18.758 --> 00:04:21.594
between wanting to support
and help Angary

00:04:21.627 --> 00:04:23.963
<i> in performing the scene,
 and having to also</i>

00:04:24.697 --> 00:04:27.700
keep so much reserved
because that's how Mare
handles the scene.

00:04:27.766 --> 00:04:28.834
I'm sorry, I'm sorry, I'm sorry.

00:04:28.867 --> 00:04:30.169
(SOBBING)

00:04:30.202 --> 00:04:33.206 line:0%
Kate, as an actor,
she's so able to just

00:04:33.539 --> 00:04:36.475
cover a gamut of emotions
and qualities

00:04:36.509 --> 00:04:39.378
<i> where you can see Mare
 being super focused</i>

00:04:39.445 --> 00:04:40.813
<i> on the work she's doing,</i>

00:04:40.846 --> 00:04:43.783
but at the same time is able to,
sort of, very closely

00:04:43.816 --> 00:04:46.018
under the surface
is a very vulnerable woman

00:04:46.051 --> 00:04:48.388
<i> who needs to be loved
 and wants to love, too.</i>

00:04:48.888 --> 00:04:51.456
Yeah, I think this was
kind of a one-time thing.

00:04:51.490 --> 00:04:52.792
Well, does it have to be?

00:04:53.159 --> 00:04:54.727
My life's complicated.

00:04:55.160 --> 00:04:58.463
It was really clear for me
to see where Richard

00:04:58.497 --> 00:05:00.066
<i> would fit into her life,</i>

00:05:00.133 --> 00:05:04.203
and what was required to be
some sort of relief for Mare.

00:05:04.971 --> 00:05:06.338
Do you ever
give yourself a break?

00:05:07.072 --> 00:05:08.641
Are you about to tell me
I look exhausted?

00:05:08.674 --> 00:05:09.976
No, but it seems like

00:05:10.009 --> 00:05:12.578
you're carrying
a lot of heavy stuff,

00:05:12.644 --> 00:05:16.048
and I, you know,
I wonder if anybody
ever does anything for you.

00:05:17.149 --> 00:05:20.353
BRAD:<i> We are dealing with dark
 and tragic events in the show.</i>

00:05:20.386 --> 00:05:22.988
But I think
in order for an audience

00:05:23.021 --> 00:05:25.324
to wanna stick around each week,

00:05:25.358 --> 00:05:28.060
it can't be too heavy
all the time.

00:05:28.094 --> 00:05:30.096
And so, the show has
a lot of humor in it.

00:05:30.663 --> 00:05:33.499
CRAIG:<i> There's a scene where
 Evan says, "Goodnight, Mare."</i>

00:05:33.532 --> 00:05:36.335
And in one of the rehearsals,
the way he delivered it,

00:05:36.368 --> 00:05:37.270
<i> he was like...</i>

00:05:37.336 --> 00:05:38.438
Have a good nightmare!

00:05:39.672 --> 00:05:41.207
Goodnight, Mare.

00:05:41.941 --> 00:05:43.242
Why don't you have a good night,
Mare?

00:05:43.276 --> 00:05:45.244 line:0%
Craig and I thought
that was absolutely hilarious.

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:46.912
And I was like, "Wait,
just say that the next time."

00:05:46.946 --> 00:05:48.881
And so,
we shot some of these things

00:05:48.914 --> 00:05:51.350
<i> that ended up in the show,
 and messing around</i>

00:05:51.384 --> 00:05:53.185
<i> and kind of finding it on set.</i>

00:05:53.619 --> 00:05:57.556
The first scene I shot was fun.
It's where we're at the funeral.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:05:59.091
Listen up!

00:05:59.124 --> 00:06:00.926
HELEN:<i> And then
 he announces out of guilt</i>

00:06:00.959 --> 00:06:03.195
in front of everyone
at his wife's wake.

00:06:03.462 --> 00:06:04.731
I had an affair...

00:06:04.897 --> 00:06:06.632
with Helen Fahey.

00:06:06.699 --> 00:06:07.733
(MARE GRUNTS)

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:10.135
BRAD:
<i>In spite of these tragic events,</i>

00:06:10.202 --> 00:06:12.137
there's a lot of laughter
in this community.

00:06:12.204 --> 00:06:13.906
-(LAUGHING)
-Li-- Li-- Listen to me.

00:06:13.939 --> 00:06:15.974
"Affair" is a strong word.
Very strong.

00:06:16.041 --> 00:06:19.812
-It happened twice.
-(LAUGHING HYSTERICALLY)

00:06:19.878 --> 00:06:21.313
Okay, three times, tops.

00:06:22.314 --> 00:06:23.750
CRAIG:
<i> The Wayne Potts sequence was</i>

00:06:23.783 --> 00:06:26.619
a particularly fun sequence
to design.

00:06:26.652 --> 00:06:29.121
<i> The location that we found was
 a real location</i>

00:06:29.155 --> 00:06:32.157
<i> that was a bar in the front
 and a house in the back.</i>

00:06:32.224 --> 00:06:33.892
The set decoration was
incredible

00:06:33.926 --> 00:06:35.728
<i> and just looked
 so real and scary.</i>

00:06:36.295 --> 00:06:39.064
KATE:<i> Logistically, we had
to really understand the space.</i>

00:06:39.098 --> 00:06:41.233
We had to understand exactly
what would happen to Evan.

00:06:41.267 --> 00:06:44.503
I think they did a countdown
like, "Three, two, one, bang!"

00:06:44.770 --> 00:06:46.172
-(GUNSHOT)
-(GRUNTS)

00:06:46.238 --> 00:06:47.640
They shot me doing that,

00:06:47.673 --> 00:06:50.643
<i> and then set up a pyrotechnic
 that had blood in it,</i>

00:06:50.676 --> 00:06:52.745
and then shot that...
(IMITATES SPLATTERING)

00:06:52.778 --> 00:06:54.013
...going on the wall.

00:06:54.646 --> 00:06:56.582
<i> And then the rest of the day,
I had a bullet hole in my face,</i>

00:06:56.616 --> 00:06:57.817
<i>and I was laying on the ground.</i>

00:06:57.850 --> 00:06:59.785
(LAUGHS) Kate was running
all around my head,

00:06:59.819 --> 00:07:02.421
and I'm like, "Please don't
step on me." (LAUGHS)

00:07:03.355 --> 00:07:06.091
I had never held a gun
before playing this part.

00:07:06.124 --> 00:07:08.027
-(GUNSHOTS)
-(MARE GASPS)

00:07:08.093 --> 00:07:11.130
We didn't want her to come
off as an action star.

00:07:11.163 --> 00:07:12.832
<i> We wanted to make sure
 that it read</i>

00:07:12.865 --> 00:07:16.335
as an unusual event
in the life of Mare Sheehan.

00:07:17.436 --> 00:07:19.938
BRAD:<i> The hardest part
 of any mystery show really is</i>

00:07:19.971 --> 00:07:21.273
<i> sticking the landing.</i>

00:07:21.306 --> 00:07:23.142
The ending has to be surprising,

00:07:23.176 --> 00:07:25.277
and yet it has to be rewarding
as well.

00:07:25.945 --> 00:07:28.147
JULIANNE NICHOLSON:
<i> I was not able to predict
 what was coming,</i>

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:30.282
which is exciting. You wanna be
a part of something

00:07:30.316 --> 00:07:32.217
where you don't know
what's coming next.

00:07:32.284 --> 00:07:35.287
I always, like, theorize
about who'd done it,

00:07:35.321 --> 00:07:36.389
and I never get it right.

00:07:36.455 --> 00:07:37.823
So I was very shocked.

00:07:37.857 --> 00:07:40.125
In a small town,
you have the opportunity

00:07:40.159 --> 00:07:41.393
to sort of investigate
all the families

00:07:41.460 --> 00:07:43.562
<i> and to see the effects
 that the crime has had</i>

00:07:43.628 --> 00:07:46.032
<i> and get involved
in everybody's personal stories.</i>

00:07:46.065 --> 00:07:48.533
The suspect is wanted
in a murder investigation.

00:07:48.867 --> 00:07:50.002
JULIANNE:<i> The truth comes out,</i>

00:07:50.035 --> 00:07:51.637
<i> and then the worst thing
 in the world</i>

00:07:51.670 --> 00:07:54.474
that you can imagine,
as a mother, happens.

00:07:55.041 --> 00:07:59.378
<i> I think that moment in the car
 where Lori blows up at Mare,</i>

00:07:59.412 --> 00:08:01.413
<i> she's been holding
 on to so much,</i>

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:04.016
<i> that it's just a moment
 where it all comes out.</i>

00:08:04.416 --> 00:08:07.586
Why couldn't you just leave it
alone? It's my Ryan!

00:08:07.653 --> 00:08:09.088
My Ryan!

00:08:09.154 --> 00:08:12.358
Lori's life is completely ruined
because Mare figures it out.

00:08:12.391 --> 00:08:15.094
CRAIG:<i> We wanted
 to make sure that landed</i>

00:08:15.161 --> 00:08:16.828
as a surprise and a twist,

00:08:16.862 --> 00:08:18.330
<i>but that you would have empathy</i>

00:08:18.364 --> 00:08:20.232
<i> for characters
 that you didn't before.</i>

00:08:20.265 --> 00:08:23.068
KATE:<i> We worked so hard
 to keep it a secret.</i>

00:08:23.102 --> 00:08:26.405
I hope that the audience
is as blown away as we all were

00:08:26.438 --> 00:08:28.341
by who the real killer is.

